I’ll inspect the diffraction pattern, metadata, and available crystallographic tooling first, then identify candidate phases and build/refine a multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the reported fractions limited to what the data can support and preserve the final refinement project.

The chemistry points naturally toward lead tungstates, but I won’t assume a single PbWO₄ product: residual PbO/WO₃ and intermediate Pb–W–O stoichiometries are plausible at 500 °C. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the installed phase-search/indexing and GSAS-II interfaces against those possibilities.

The peak pattern is not single-phase. The dominant reflections form a coherent tetragonal scheelite PbWO₄ series; two weaker signatures are also visible: the characteristic WO₃ low-angle triplet near 23–25° and orthorhombic PbO reflections near 15°, 29–31°, and 38°. I’m testing these three models quantitatively and checking whether monoclinic PbWO₄ explains any peaks better than the precursor oxides.

The two-phase tests confirm both residual precursor signatures are real: adding massicot PbO lowers Rwp from 18.35% to 16.28%, while adding monoclinic WO₃ alone lowers it to 17.33%. I’m now testing the combined three-phase model and alternatives for the remaining unmatched peaks; the key question is whether a fourth lead-tungstate phase is warranted or would merely absorb profile errors.

The three-phase refinement reaches Rwp = 15.08% and gives roughly 78.5% scheelite PbWO₄, 9.8% massicot PbO, and 11.7% monoclinic WO₃. It also exposes a coherent set of still-unmodeled reflections (notably 25.91°, 30.15°, and 31.05°), so I’m indexing that residual set and searching for a chemically plausible fourth Pb–W–O phase rather than accepting an incomplete model.

The residual phase is now identified convincingly as Pb₂WO₅: an isostructural Pb₂MoO₅ model with Mo→W reproduces the unmatched peak series, and adding it lowers Rwp from 15.08% to 12.71%. The refined monoclinic cell (C2/m, a≈14.230, b≈5.811, c≈7.361 Å, β≈113.88°) remains close to the analog structure. I’m checking each minor phase’s necessity and then will refine the final model conservatively, including phase-specific broadening only where supported.

All four phases remain independently required: omitting WO₃ raises Rwp to 14.23%, omitting PbO raises it to 14.64%, and omitting Pb₂WO₅ raises it to 15.08%. Two equivalent monoclinic WO₃ settings give nearly identical fractions and fit quality; I’m using the structural variant supported best by the full profile and checking the lower-symmetry alternative before finalizing.

The pattern resolves into four crystalline components: scheelite PbWO₄, massicot PbO, monoclinic WO₃, and Pb₂WO₅. I’m finishing the stable GSAS-II refinement now; the main remaining judgment is whether a small instrumental-profile improvement is robust enough to retain without destabilizing the phase fractions.

The more complete profile model converged cleanly and leaves the phase fractions essentially unchanged (all shifts below 0.13 wt%). I’m retaining that model because the axial-divergence and polarization terms are physically plausible and improve the fit without introducing the unstable crystallite-size behavior seen in exploratory refinements.

The final full-range refinement is converged: Rwp = 12.57%, with crystalline fractions 67.88% PbWO₄, 14.52% Pb₂WO₅, 10.36% WO₃, and 7.24% PbO. I’ve generated the project, profiles, and CIFs; I’m validating that every CIF re-imports correctly and that the JSON/profile values exactly match the saved GSAS-II project before writing the uncertainty note.

